<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Ainu chiefdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1869 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The self-governing communities of the Ainu, the indigenous people of Hokkaido, Sakhalin and the Kuril Islands, before Japanese annexation in 1869.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> culture took recognisable form around the 13th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>, growing out of the earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsumon-culture">Satsumon culture</a> with influences from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okhotsk-culture">Okhotsk culture</a>. Ainu also lived in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a>, the Kurils and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. They called their land Ainu Mosir, the land of humans.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>There was no single state. Villages (kotan) led by elders formed regional groupings under powerful chiefs, who built hilltop forts called chashi. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> lived by hunting, fishing and trade, exchanging salmon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and hawks for Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, iron and lacquer and for Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> via the Amur. Religion centred on kamuy spirits, honoured in rites such as the bear-sending ceremony.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>An uprising against Japanese traders under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koshamain">Koshamain</a> in 1457 failed, and after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/matsumae-domain">Matsumae Domain</a> imposed a trade monopoly, so did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shakushain">Shakushain's</a> war of 1669. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Meiji Japan</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a> in 1869 and pursued assimilation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a> as an indigenous people in 2019.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1300 CE – 1869 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Korea and Japan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:11.32%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1869 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Koshamain, Shakushain</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ainu animism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ainu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ainu Mosir, Ainu kotan chiefdoms</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=ainu-chiefdoms" aria-label="Ainu chiefdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8167 873 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ainu chiefdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8418 957h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8487 1129h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8487 1129h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=ainu-chiefdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

